<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The Microsoft Visual C++ x64 runtime that PyTorch needs, merged into the MSI.

  PyTorch's Windows wheels cannot load torch\lib\c10.dll without a current Visual C++
  2015-2022 x64 runtime ([WinError 1114]), and a fresh Windows does not always have one.
  Microsoft ships the runtime as a merge module (Microsoft_VC14x_CRT_x64.msm) with Visual
  Studio, for exactly this: an MSI that carries it installs it with Windows Installer's
  own rules. It needs no network at install time, and Windows Installer never replaces a
  newer vcruntime140.dll or msvcp140.dll than the one in the module, so a machine that
  already has a newer runtime keeps it and the installation still succeeds. The files are
  shared, reference-counted components in System32: uninstalling MuMDIA leaves them for
  every other program that also installed them.

  Used only through tauri.vcruntime.conf.json, which the release and CI builds pass with
  `cargo tauri build --config`. The module's path comes from TAURI_MUMDIA_VC_CRT_MSM
  (the bundler passes only TAURI_* variables to WiX), which ci/find_vc_merge_module.ps1
  sets from the Visual Studio installation on the build machine. A plain
  `cargo tauri build` does not read this file and builds an MSI without the runtime.
  desktop/README.md, "The Visual C++ runtime", has the reasoning and the licence.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/wix/2006/wi">
  <Fragment>
    <DirectoryRef Id="TARGETDIR">
      <Merge Id="VCRuntimeX64" SourceFile="$(env.TAURI_MUMDIA_VC_CRT_MSM)" DiskId="1" Language="0" />
    </DirectoryRef>
  </Fragment>
</Wix>
